Name: matches

Type: operator

Syntax: <string> matches wildcard [pattern] <wildcardPattern>

Syntax: <string> matches regex [pattern] <regexPattern>

Summary:
Returns true if a string matches a wildcard pattern or a regular
expression, and false if it does not.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, android, html5

Platforms: desktop, server, mobile

Example:
"notes.txt" matches wildcard "*.txt" -- evaluates to true

Example:
"notes.txt.bak" matches wildcard "*.txt" -- evaluates to false

Example:
"2026-10-24" matches regex "^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$" -- evaluates to true

Example:
local tFile, tImages
repeat for each line tFile in files(specialFolderPath("documents"))
   if tFile matches wildcard "*.png" then
      put tFile & return after tImages
   end if
end repeat

Parameters:
string (string):
The text to match.

wildcardPattern (string):
A pattern of the kind the <filter> <command> takes, with `*`, `?` and
`[chars]`.

regexPattern (string):
A <regular expression>, as the <matchText> <function> takes.

Description:
Use the <matches> <operator> to test a string against a pattern where
an <if> <control structure> or any other <expression> needs true or
false.

`matches wildcard` is true when the whole of <string> matches
<wildcardPattern>, which is read as the <filter> <command> reads one:
`*` matches any characters or none, `?` any one character, and `[abc]`,
`[a-c]` and `[!abc]` one of a set of characters (or one not in it). It
takes the <caseSensitive> <property> into account, as <filter> does.

`matches regex` is true when <regexPattern> matches <string>, as the
<matchText> <function> would say: it can match anywhere in the string,
unless the pattern begins with `^` or ends with `$`, and it matches
case, whatever the <caseSensitive>, unless the pattern begins with
`(?i)`. A <regexPattern> that is not a <regular expression> is an
error, as it is in <matchText>.

The word `pattern` may follow `wildcard` or `regex`, as it may in the
<filter> <command>.

The <matches> <operator> binds as the <contains> and <begins with>
operators do: after `&`, and before `is`, <and> and <or>, so
`tName matches wildcard tPrefix & "*" and tSize > 0` needs no brackets.

`matches` is not a reserved word: a <variable>, a <handler> or a
<custom property> named `matches` works as it always has. It is read as
this <operator> only after a value and before `wildcard` or `regex`.

A few lines meant something else before the <matches> <operator> was
added: `get tText matches wildcard (tPattern)` was `get tText`, then a
call of a <handler> named `matches` with the value of a <function>
named `wildcard`, and `return tText matches wildcard (tPattern)`
returned tText. So that scripts work as they did, a <handler> that
compiles without the <matches> <operator> is read as before, every line
of it, and only one that does not compile without it is read with it;
so is a <do> script or a <value> <expression>. Any other use of the
<operator>, such as those in the examples above, did not compile
before, so a <handler> that has one is read with the <operator>. To
have the <operator> in a line such as
`get tText matches wildcard (tPattern)`, leave out the brackets around
the pattern, or put them around the whole <expression>:
`get (tText matches wildcard tPattern)`.

The <matches> <operator> comes from HyperXTalk, so that stacks written
for it work unchanged.

References: filter (command), matchText (function),
matchChunk (function), contains (operator), begins with (operator),
ends with (operator), = (operator), and (operator), or (operator),
if (control structure), do (command), value (function),
caseSensitive (property), regular expression (glossary),
expression (glossary), property (glossary), variable (glossary),
handler (glossary), custom property (glossary)

Tags: text processing
